\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
1
\series default
.
 We are asked to calculate the Fisher information matrix of the normal distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p(x|\mu)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp(-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2})$
\end_inset

 (which will be a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times1$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

matrix
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for a one-parameter model like this).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is the Fisher information matrix?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fisher information measures how much information a random variable conveys about model parameters.
 If a small change to the parameter changes the distribution a lot,
 then observing a sample from the distribution tells you a lot about the parameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The partial derivative (with respect to parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

) of the log-likelihood is called the 
\emph on
score
\emph default
.
 Given some boring regularity conditions,
 the expectation of the score at the true parameter value is 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

.
 The Fisher information is the variance of the score.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{Var}\left(\ddth\log f(X;\theta)\right)=E\left[\mathrm{score}^{2}\right]-\cancelto{0}{E\left[\mathrm{score}\right]^{2}}=E_{X\sim P_{\theta}}\left[\left(\ddth\log f(X;\theta)\right)^{2}\right]=\int\left(\ddth\log f(X;\theta)\right)^{2}f(x;\theta)\,dx
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GPT-5.6 Sol writes out the example of a Bernoulli distribution for us (so that I'm not spoiled for the normal,
 which was the actual exercise).
 The Bernoulli with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x;p)=p^{x}(1-p)^{1-x}$
\end_inset

.
 Take the logarithm:
 that's 
\begin_inset Formula $\log p^{x}(1-p)^{1-x}=x\log p+(1-x)\log(1-p)$
\end_inset

.
 Then differentiate with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

:
 that's 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{x}{p}-\frac{1-x}{1-p}=\frac{(1-p)x-p(1-x)}{p(1-p)}=\frac{x\cancel{-px}-p\cancel{+px}}{p(1-p)}=\frac{x-p}{p(1-p)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we square and take the expectation:
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{X\sim\mathrm{Bern}(p)}\left[\frac{\left(x-p\right)^{2}}{p^{2}(1-p)^{2}}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 The denominator doesn't depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 so we're at 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{E\left[\left(x-p\right)^{2}\right]}{p^{2}(1-p)^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\left(x-p\right)^{2}\right]$
\end_inset

 is the variance (because in this case,
 the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is the mean),
 and the variance for the Bernoulli is 
\begin_inset Formula $p(1-p)$
\end_inset

,
 so we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{p(1-p)}{p^{2}(1-p)^{2}}=\frac{1}{p(1-p)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's try the normal now.
 We start with 
\begin_inset Formula $p(x|\mu)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp(-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2})$
\end_inset

 and take the logarithm 
\begin_inset Formula $\log\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp(-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2})\right)=\log\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\right)+\cancel{\log\exp}\left(-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 so we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\log\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 but the constant term drops out when we differentiate with respect to the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\dd{\mu}\left(-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2}+C\right)=-\cancel{\frac{2}{2}}(x-\mu)\cdot-1=x-\mu$
\end_inset

.
 Then we square and take the expectation.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{X\sim\mathrm{Norm(\mu,1)}}\left[\left(X-\mu\right)^{2}\right]=\int\left(X-\mu\right)^{2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp(-\frac{1}{2}(x-\mu)^{2})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
...
 and it's not clear to me what I'm supposed to do with this integral.
 GPT-5.6 Sol (prompted for a minimal hint) points out that we don't need it—

\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\left(X-\mu\right)^{2}\right]$
\end_inset

 is the definition of variance.
 
\end_layout

